{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780782348088,"sessionID":"ses_1611a223effeVm9QJZLOjnSHiy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_8lKkuP2Y8lu7mPCEPQC0ZIL0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/agents/proof-risk-auditor.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/agents/proof-risk-auditor.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: description: 'Audit proposal and RCA fix-decision proof plans for runtime-claim evidence-class mismatch.'\n3: model: gpt-xhigh\n4: output_format: ''\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Proof Risk Auditor\n8: \n9: ## Declared roles\n10: \n11: `validator`, `parser`\n12: \n13: ## Role\n14: \n15: You are a read-only critic for ACR-254 proof risk. You inspect a Phase 3 proposal or RCA fix-decision artifact and decide whether its `## Proof plan` names a runtime claim, names a proof method, and explains why the proof method's evidence class exercises that runtime claim rather than a proxy surface.\n16: \n17: You are a critic, not a proposer. Do not revise the proposal, write replacement proof-plan text, edit code, edit tests, dispatch implementation work, or create evidence. Write only the caller-supplied `report_path`.\n18: \n19: ## Use When\n20: \n21: - Phase 4 needs an independent proposal-risk gate over a Phase 3 proposal.\n22: - Code-quality or Phase 8 has proposal/proof-plan/runtime-claim context and needs proof-risk evidence preserved beside actual-diff review.\n23: - RCA fix-decision proposes a fix and must prove that the planned verification surface matches the runtime claim before application planning.\n24: \n25: ## Do Not Use When\n26: \n27: - The caller needs actual PR-diff or RCA dossier validation-surface weakening detection; use `agents/validation-integrity-auditor.md`.\n28: - The caller needs uncovered-code risk/value ranking; use `agents/risk-assessor.md`.\n29: - The caller needs test-quality, coverage, or spec-alignment synthesis; use `agents/test-audit-gate.md`.\n30: - The caller wants proof-plan authoring, proposal revision, runtime-artifact execution, or code fixes.\n31: \n32: ## Inputs\n33: \n34: - `mode=<phase-3-proposal|rca-fix-decision>` (required) - selects the artifact context.\n35: - `proposal_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - Phase 3 proposal or RCA fix-decision artifact under review.\n36: - `report_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - Markdown report destination; this is the only path you write.\n37: - `worktree_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - repository worktree used to resolve relative evidence paths.\n38: - `contract_path=<absolute-path>` (required for Phase 6 per-component code-quality) - Step 6a contract used to resolve declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, runtime obligations, fixture application points, and proof scope before judging the proof plan. Missing or unreadable `contract_path` in Phase 6 is `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`.\n39: \n40: Missing required inputs produce `BLOCKED:<reason>`. A malformed or insufficient proof plan produces a non-LOW verdict, not an automatic stop state, unless the artifact cannot be read or parsed at all.\n41: \n42: ## Procedure\n43: \n44: 1. Load `mode`, `proposal_path`, `report_path`, and `worktree_path`. Fail closed with `BLOCKED:missing-required-input` or `BLOCKED:unreadable-input` when required inputs are absent or unreadable. Resolve relative evidence paths against `worktree_path`; do not assume the current working directory is the worktree.\n45: 2. In Phase 6 per-component code-quality, read `contract_path` before scoring. If `contract_path` is missing, unreadable, or blank, return `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path` instead of judging proof risk from generic context.\n46: 3. Record the file size and a short sha256 excerpt for `proposal_path` and, when supplied, `contract_path`. Do not write anywhere except `report_path`.\n47: 4. Parse `proposal_path` for an exact `## Proof plan` section.\n48: 5. Verify that `## Proof plan` contains all required fields:\n49:    - `Runtime claim`: one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix.\n50:    - `Proof method`: the validation surface or surfaces that will exercise the runtime claim.\n51:    - `Evidence-class match`: an explicit statement explaining why the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class.\n52: 6. Emit `PR-<NNN>` findings for missing proof-plan structure:\n53:    - Missing `## Proof plan` is `HIGH`.\n54:    - Missing runtime claim is `HIGH`.\n55:    - Missing proof method is `HIGH`.\n56:    - Missing evidence-class match is `HIGH`.\n57:    - Self-certification, such as saying the proof plan itself is the validation or that tests pass without naming a validation surface, is `HIGH`.\n58: 7. Classify the runtime claim's required evidence class, using the Step 6a contract and proposal together in Phase 6:\n59:    - Runtime-artifact-bound claims name production artifacts, containers, deployed services, production-path commands, stateful migrations, runtime startup, real dependency availability, real external adapters, or built-image behavior.\n60:    - Proxy-layer claims explicitly name test harness behavior, mock behavior, local fixture behavior, static schema parsing, documentation shape, or another non-runtime layer as the actual target.\n61: 8. Classify the proof method:\n62:    - Proxy-only proof includes only tests pass, test-environment import, host-only check, mocked dependency, stubbed fixture, fake service, relaxed schema, static check, or equivalent proxy evidence.\n63:    - Runtime-artifact proof includes build plus run plus verify of the named artifact, container execution, production entrypoint, production-shaped migration target, deployed environment check, state DB evidence, or a project-specific runtime artifact that matches the claim.\n64:    - Mixed proof is acceptable only when proxy evidence is scoped to proxy behavior and runtime claims receive explicit runtime-artifact evidence-class matching.\n65: 9. Detect evidence-class mismatch:\n66:    - A container startup/import claim cannot be validated only by a test-environment import.\n67:    - A production DB migration claim cannot be validated only by mock DB success.\n68:    - A deployed-service behavior claim cannot be validated only by unit tests or fixture success.\n69: 10. Assign verdicts:\n70:    - `HIGH` for missing required proof-plan fields, self-certification, proxy-only proof for runtime-artifact-bound claims, or evidence-class mismatch.\n71:    - `LOW` for direct runtime-artifact proof that matches the runtime claim.\n72:    - `LOW` for proxy proof when the runtime claim is genuinely a proxy-layer claim and the evidence-class match says so.\n73:    - `LOW` for mixed proxy and runtime proof when the proof plan explicitly scopes proxy evidence and binds runtime claims to runtime-artifact evidence.\n74:    - `MEDIUM` only for partially ambiguous evidence-class wording where the artifact is named but the matching statement is incomplete and no HIGH condition fires.\n75: 11. Write the report to `report_path` and return the terminal verdict on the last non-blank line of the report and on stdout.\n76: \n77: ## Report Format\n78: \n79: Report skeleton:\n80: \n81: ```md\n82: # Proof-risk audit report\n83: \n84: ## Inputs read\n85: | Input | Path or value | Size | SHA excerpt | Notes |\n86: |---|---|---:|---|---|\n87: \n88: ## Proof-plan parse\n89: | Field | Present | Evidence |\n90: |---|---:|---|\n91: \n92: ## Findings\n93: | Finding ID | Severity | Runtime claim | Proof method | Proxy class | Required runtime artifact | Evidence refs | Blocks pipeline |\n94: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n95: \n96: ## Evidence-class decision\n97: \n98: ## Residual ambiguity / stop-condition notes\n99: \n100: <terminal verdict>\n101: ```\n102: \n103: Finding records must include `id`, `severity`, `runtime_claim`, `proof_plan_ref`, `proof_method`, `proxy_class`, `required_runtime_artifact`, `evidence_refs`, and `blocks_pipeline`.\n104: \n105: ## Verdict\n106: \n107: Use exactly this vocabulary:\n108: \n109: - `LOW` when proof-plan structure is complete and the proof method's evidence class matches the claim.\n110: - `MEDIUM` when evidence-class wording is incomplete but no missing-field, self-certification, proxy-only, or mismatch finding fires.\n111: - `HIGH` when any missing required proof-plan field, self-certification, proxy-only runtime proof, or evidence-class mismatch remains.\n112: - `NEEDS_INPUT:<absolute_artifact_path>` only for a genuine human-owned ambiguity that cannot be resolved from the supplied artifact and materially changes the verdict.\n113: - `BLOCKED:<reason>` for missing required inputs, unreadable required artifacts, unparseable proposal text, or unwritable `report_path`.\n114: \n115: The final non-blank report line and final stdout token must be the verdict.\n116: \n117: ## Stop Conditions\n118: \n119: - Success: report written with `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, or `HIGH`.\n120: - `BLOCKED:missing-required-input`: `mode`, `proposal_path`, `report_path`, or `worktree_path` is missing.\n121: - `BLOCKED:unreadable-input`: `proposal_path` cannot be read.\n122: - `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`: Phase 6 requires `contract_path` and it cannot be read before scoring.\n123: - `BLOCKED:malformed-mode`: `mode` is not `phase-3-proposal` or `rca-fix-decision`.\n124: - `BLOCKED:unwritable-output`: `report_path` cannot be written.\n125: - `NEEDS_INPUT:<absolute_artifact_path>`: only for a root-owned question artifact about an unresolved claim/evidence identity conflict, not for missing proof-plan fields.\n126: \n127: ## Sibling Boundaries\n128: \n129: - `agents/risk-assessor.md` ranks uncovered code by outage potential, blast radius, and business value. This operator evaluates proof-plan evidence-class matching.\n130: - `agents/validation-integrity-auditor.md` inspects actual diffs and RCA verification evidence for validation weakening after implementation or application.\n131: - `agents/test-audit-gate.md` synthesizes spec alignment, test quality, and coverage delta; it does not replace proof-risk review.\n132: - A proposal can be proof-risk LOW and still fail scope, supported-surface, audit, shortcut, or code-quality gates.\n\n(End of file - total 132 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ndescription: 'Audit proposal and RCA fix-decision proof plans for runtime-claim evidence-class mismatch.'\nmodel: gpt-xhigh\noutput_format: ''\n---\n\n# Proof Risk Auditor\n\n## Declared roles\n\n`validator`, `parser`\n\n## Role\n\nYou are a read-only critic for ACR-254 proof risk. You inspect a Phase 3 proposal or RCA fix-decision artifact and decide whether its `## Proof plan` names a runtime claim, names a proof method, and explains why the proof method's evidence class exercises that runtime claim rather than a proxy surface.\n\nYou are a critic, not a proposer. Do not revise the proposal, write replacement proof-plan text, edit code, edit tests, dispatch implementation work, or create evidence. Write only the caller-supplied `report_path`.\n\n## Use When\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/agents/proof-risk-auditor.md","text":"---\ndescription: 'Audit proposal and RCA fix-decision proof plans for runtime-claim evidence-class mismatch.'\nmodel: gpt-xhigh\noutput_format: ''\n---\n\n# Proof Risk Auditor\n\n## Declared roles\n\n`validator`, `parser`\n\n## Role\n\nYou are a read-only critic for ACR-254 proof risk. You inspect a Phase 3 proposal or RCA fix-decision artifact and decide whether its `## Proof plan` names a runtime claim, names a proof method, and explains why the proof method's evidence class exercises that runtime claim rather than a proxy surface.\n\nYou are a critic, not a proposer. Do not revise the proposal, write replacement proof-plan text, edit code, edit tests, dispatch implementation work, or create evidence. Write only the caller-supplied `report_path`.\n\n## Use When\n\n- Phase 4 needs an independent proposal-risk gate over a Phase 3 proposal.\n- Code-quality or Phase 8 has proposal/proof-plan/runtime-claim context and needs proof-risk evidence preserved beside actual-diff review.\n- RCA fix-decision proposes a fix and must prove that the planned verification surface matches the runtime claim before application planning.\n\n## Do Not Use When\n\n- The caller needs actual PR-diff or RCA dossier validation-surface weakening detection; use `agents/validation-integrity-auditor.md`.\n- The caller needs uncovered-code risk/value ranking; use `agents/risk-assessor.md`.\n- The caller needs test-quality, coverage, or spec-alignment synthesis; use `agents/test-audit-gate.md`.\n- The caller wants proof-plan authoring, proposal revision, runtime-artifact execution, or code fixes.\n\n## Inputs\n\n- `mode=<phase-3-proposal|rca-fix-decision>` (required) - selects the artifact context.\n- `proposal_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - Phase 3 proposal or RCA fix-decision artifact under review.\n- `report_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - Markdown report destination; this is the only path you write.\n- `worktree_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - repository worktree used to resolve relative evidence paths.\n- `contract_path=<absolute-path>` (required for Phase 6 per-component code-quality) - Step 6a contract used to resolve declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, runtime obligations, fixture application points, and proof scope before judging the proof plan. Missing or unreadable `contract_path` in Phase 6 is `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`.\n\nMissing required inputs produce `BLOCKED:<reason>`. A malformed or insufficient proof plan produces a non-LOW verdict, not an automatic stop state, unless the artifact cannot be read or parsed at all.\n\n## Procedure\n\n1. Load `mode`, `proposal_path`, `report_path`, and `worktree_path`. Fail closed with `BLOCKED:missing-required-input` or `BLOCKED:unreadable-input` when required inputs are absent or unreadable. Resolve relative evidence paths against `worktree_path`; do not assume the current working directory is the worktree.\n2. In Phase 6 per-component code-quality, read `contract_path` before scoring. If `contract_path` is missing, unreadable, or blank, return `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path` instead of judging proof risk from generic context.\n3. Record the file size and a short sha256 excerpt for `proposal_path` and, when supplied, `contract_path`. Do not write anywhere except `report_path`.\n4. Parse `proposal_path` for an exact `## Proof plan` section.\n5. Verify that `## Proof plan` contains all required fields:\n   - `Runtime claim`: one or more sentences naming the runtime behavior asserted by the fix.\n   - `Proof method`: the validation surface or surfaces that will exercise the runtime claim.\n   - `Evidence-class match`: an explicit statement explaining why the proof method's evidence class matches the runtime claim's required evidence class.\n6. Emit `PR-<NNN>` findings for missing proof-plan structure:\n   - Missing `## Proof plan` is `HIGH`.\n   - Missing runtime claim is `HIGH`.\n   - Missing proof method is `HIGH`.\n   - Missing evidence-class match is `HIGH`.\n   - Self-certification, such as saying the proof plan itself is the validation or that tests pass without naming a validation surface, is `HIGH`.\n7. Classify the runtime claim's required evidence class, using the Step 6a contract and proposal together in Phase 6:\n   - Runtime-artifact-bound claims name production artifacts, containers, deployed services, production-path commands, stateful migrations, runtime startup, real dependency availability, real external adapters, or built-image behavior.\n   - Proxy-layer claims explicitly name test harness behavior, mock behavior, local fixture behavior, static schema parsing, documentation shape, or another non-runtime layer as the actual target.\n8. Classify the proof method:\n   - Proxy-only proof includes only tests pass, test-environment import, host-only check, mocked dependency, stubbed fixture, fake service, relaxed schema, static check, or equivalent proxy evidence.\n   - Runtime-artifact proof includes build plus run plus verify of the named artifact, container execution, production entrypoint, production-shaped migration target, deployed environment check, state DB evidence, or a project-specific runtime artifact that matches the claim.\n   - Mixed proof is acceptable only when proxy evidence is scoped to proxy behavior and runtime claims receive explicit runtime-artifact evidence-class matching.\n9. Detect evidence-class mismatch:\n   - A container startup/import claim cannot be validated only by a test-environment import.\n   - A production DB migration claim cannot be validated only by mock DB success.\n   - A deployed-service behavior claim cannot be validated only by unit tests or fixture success.\n10. Assign verdicts:\n   - `HIGH` for missing required proof-plan fields, self-certification, proxy-only proof for runtime-artifact-bound claims, or evidence-class mismatch.\n   - `LOW` for direct runtime-artifact proof that matches the runtime claim.\n   - `LOW` for proxy proof when the runtime claim is genuinely a proxy-layer claim and the evidence-class match says so.\n   - `LOW` for mixed proxy and runtime proof when the proof plan explicitly scopes proxy evidence and binds runtime claims to runtime-artifact evidence.\n   - `MEDIUM` only for partially ambiguous evidence-class wording where the artifact is named but the matching statement is incomplete and no HIGH condition fires.\n11. Write the report to `report_path` and return the terminal verdict on the last non-blank line of the report and on stdout.\n\n## Report Format\n\nReport skeleton:\n\n```md\n# Proof-risk audit report\n\n## Inputs read\n| Input | Path or value | Size | SHA excerpt | Notes |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|\n\n## Proof-plan parse\n| Field | Present | Evidence |\n|---|---:|---|\n\n## Findings\n| Finding ID | Severity | Runtime claim | Proof method | Proxy class | Required runtime artifact | Evidence refs | Blocks pipeline |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Evidence-class decision\n\n## Residual ambiguity / stop-condition notes\n\n<terminal verdict>\n```\n\nFinding records must include `id`, `severity`, `runtime_claim`, `proof_plan_ref`, `proof_method`, `proxy_class`, `required_runtime_artifact`, `evidence_refs`, and `blocks_pipeline`.\n\n## Verdict\n\nUse exactly this vocabulary:\n\n- `LOW` when proof-plan structure is complete and the proof method's evidence class matches the claim.\n- `MEDIUM` when evidence-class wording is incomplete but no missing-field, self-certification, proxy-only, or mismatch finding fires.\n- `HIGH` when any missing required proof-plan field, self-certification, proxy-only runtime proof, or evidence-class mismatch remains.\n- `NEEDS_INPUT:<absolute_artifact_path>` only for a genuine human-owned ambiguity that cannot be resolved from the supplied artifact and materially changes the verdict.\n- `BLOCKED:<reason>` for missing required inputs, unreadable required artifacts, unparseable proposal text, or unwritable `report_path`.\n\nThe final non-blank report line and final stdout token must be the verdict.\n\n## Stop Conditions\n\n- Success: report written with `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, or `HIGH`.\n- `BLOCKED:missing-required-input`: `mode`, `proposal_path`, `report_path`, or `worktree_path` is missing.\n- `BLOCKED:unreadable-input`: `proposal_path` cannot be read.\n- `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`: Phase 6 requires `contract_path` and it cannot be read before scoring.\n- `BLOCKED:malformed-mode`: `mode` is not `phase-3-proposal` or `rca-fix-decision`.\n- `BLOCKED:unwritable-output`: `report_path` cannot be written.\n- `NEEDS_INPUT:<absolute_artifact_path>`: only for a root-owned question artifact about an unresolved claim/evidence identity conflict, not for missing proof-plan fields.\n\n## Sibling Boundaries\n\n- `agents/risk-assessor.md` ranks uncovered code by outage potential, blast radius, and business value. This operator evaluates proof-plan evidence-class matching.\n- `agents/validation-integrity-auditor.md` inspects actual diffs and RCA verification evidence for validation weakening after implementation or application.\n- `agents/test-audit-gate.md` synthesizes spec alignment, test quality, and coverage delta; it does not replace proof-risk review.\n- A proposal can be proof-risk LOW and still fail scope, supported-surface, audit, shortcut, or code-quality gates.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":132,"totalLines":132,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../home/nes/ai/agents/proof-risk-auditor.md","time":{"start":1780782348020,"end":1780782348076}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_02a7eae52b1f1412016a24950bf4e08193a75b87fade9456d2"}},"id":"prt_e9ee636ce001YTTeiXme5OoXw4","sessionID":"ses_1611a223effeVm9QJZLOjnSHiy","messageID":"msg_e9ee5ded00018U4MXfBBrZzlIy"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780782348088,"sessionID":"ses_1611a223effeVm9QJZLOjnSHiy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_anxjHHl1bxxrcBuPY7zwdGKl","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/proposal.md","offset":1,"limit":4000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/proposal.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # S11 Gate Proposal\n2: \n3: S11 completes the external-provider wake cutover. The delta makes detached wake resumes use the same selected provider settings, policy, launch environment, registry root, and captured provider session identity as the original external launch. It also stops treating a spawned wake as delivered until delivery is confirmed by provider-ingested turn evidence or by a signed submitted-turn marker.\n4: \n5: The implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. New wake ownership and delivery-attempt state lives in the existing sidecar mailbox/pid-identity database, while state-db reads are limited to existing session-turn/body data. The audited functional cutoff is `95699d6..1593ef8`; the audited net source includes the post-cutoff mode-only remediation `1e043d3`, because the pinned cutoff tracked `scripts/opencode-turns` as non-executable and could not pass the workspace test suite.\n6: \n7: Live evidence exists for external wake dispatch, spawned detached wake, resumed notification delivery, and successful xhigh external smoke. Live S10 files prove external launch smoke, while deterministic S10 tests remain the binding resume-compatibility proof.\n8: \n9: ## Proof plan\n10: \n11: Evidence log: `planning/s11-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n12: \n13: Runtime claim: External provider launch records the provider child process identity and captured provider session id so `agent-messenger notify` can resolve the sidecar owner and spawn a detached wake.\n14: \n15: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_launch_notify_uses_captured_sidecar_owner_and_wakes` plus live `/tmp/s11-e2e/initial10.log` and `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-notify2.stdout`.\n16: \n17: Evidence-class match: full CLI/external-provider integration plus live smoke. The shipped test launches through the external provider fixture, captures sidecar identity, enqueues a notification, and asserts wake spawn. The live logs show `DISPATCHED ab_19e9eb3c740_11963_58a0b4405542b714`, successful initial `OULIPOLY_RESULT`, and wake `status: spawned` with `auto_wake_count: 1`.\n18: \n19: Runtime claim: A headless wake is not marked delivered merely because resume ran; delivery is confirmed only when the resumed provider output yields a matching submitted-turn marker or exact ingested user turn evidence.\n20: \n21: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_wake_does_not_mark_delivered_when_resume_produces_no_turn`, `::external_provider_wake_confirms_delivery_from_submitted_turn_marker`, `::external_provider_wake_ignores_submitted_turn_marker_for_different_payload`, `src-tauri/tests/wu_b_mailbox_integration.rs::resume_marks_delivered_from_exact_ingested_user_turn_without_assistant_delta`, and `::resume_rejects_different_ingested_user_turn_without_assistant_delta`.\n22: \n23: Evidence-class match: integration tests exercise the exact resume/mailbox delivery path and state assertions, not a proxy. They cover the negative no-turn path, positive nonce/hash marker confirmation, wrong-payload rejection, and built-in exact-user-turn confirmation.\n24: \n25: Runtime claim: Failed or rate-limited external wake attempts remain pending, record delivery-attempt/error evidence, release the wake claim, and can be retried.\n26: \n27: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_failed_wake_releases_claim_and_retries_pending_mailbox`, `::external_provider_rate_limited_wake_records_error_and_retries_pending_mailbox`, and `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs::tests::mark_delivery_failed_records_attempt_without_delivery`.\n28: \n29: Evidence-class match: full wake integration plus sidecar unit coverage. The tests assert `delivery_attempts` increments, `delivered_at` remains absent on failure, `delivery_error` records the failure class, claims are released, and the pending mailbox row is eligible for retry.\n30: \n31: Runtime claim: External-provider policy and launch requests carry the selected provider settings identity, hybrid launch shape, host linkage environment, and account-specific OpenCode auth while excluding ambient OpenAI/XDG leakage.\n32: \n33: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/age217_s6a_policy_launch_dispatch.rs::external_provider_policy_evaluate_runs_before_launch_and_uses_selected_provider_settings`, `::external_provider_policy_request_passes_hybrid_launch_shape`, `::external_provider_launch_env_carries_host_linkage_without_openai_keys`, `::external_provider_launch_env_uses_selected_opencode_auth_context_without_openai_keys`, `::external_provider_launch_env_omits_ambient_xdg_for_default_opencode_auth_context`, and `::external_provider_policy_rejection_skips_launch`.\n34: \n35: Evidence-class match: executor/provider-client integration fixtures capture the actual policy and launch protocol requests, environment maps, and rejection branch. This directly validates the request-shaping and environment boundary being claimed.\n36: \n37: Runtime claim: Detached wake resume reloads the external provider registry from the launch-time model/config roots and can use ingested external sessions when the original launch capture is missing.\n38: \n39: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_launch_notify_uses_captured_sidecar_owner_and_wakes` and `::external_provider_runtime_uses_ingested_session_when_launch_capture_missing`.\n40: \n41: Evidence-class match: CLI/external-provider integration uses isolated model/config/data roots and proves the wake path supplies the correct models dir and session source. The fallback test exercises the runtime path where launch capture is absent but ingested external session metadata is available.\n42: \n43: Runtime claim: S11 preserves S10 external provider launch/resume compatibility.\n44: \n45: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`, `::external_launch_session_id_alias_persists_external_capture_method_without_session_capability`, and live `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log`, `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log`, `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-nes-projects-agent-runner/45ccb26a-8bb6-4486-9b1e-2226e29292a0/tasks/b1vgap7ge.output`.\n46: \n47: Evidence-class match: deterministic CLI integration remains the binding resume proof and asserts external launch resume with recorded cwd and capture rows. Live smoke is scoped to external launch success only: the files contain `S10-EXTERNAL-OK` or `S10-FINAL-OK` and successful result markers.\n48: \n49: Runtime claim: The external xhigh route still reaches the external provider path after S11.\n50: \n51: Proof method: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-nes-projects-agent-runner/45ccb26a-8bb6-4486-9b1e-2226e29292a0/tasks/bvnh4nja0.output`.\n52: \n53: Evidence-class match: live runtime smoke. The retained task output records `gpt-xhigh flipped`, `=== smoke: gpt-xhigh through external ===`, `XHIGH-EXTERNAL-OK`, and `\"status\":\"succeeded\"`.\n54: \n55: Runtime claim: Live S11 wake delivery reached the resumed provider and the sidecar recorded confirmed delivery in one attempt.\n56: \n57: Proof method: `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-opencode-argv.log`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-xdg-data/oulipoly-agent-runner/pid-identity.db`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-xdg-data/oulipoly-agent-runner/invocations/7a46a1a5-844d-45e8-bd67-aecaf9cf9194.result`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh13-export.json`, and workload marker files under `/tmp/s11-e2e`.\n58: \n59: Evidence-class match: live sidecar/runtime artifacts. The argv log shows the resumed prompt with `[OULIPOLY NOTIFICATIONS]`; the mailbox rows have `delivered_at` set, `delivered_by_invocation_uuid='7a46a1a5-844d-45e8-bd67-aecaf9cf9194'`, `delivery_attempts=1`, and `delivery_error=NULL`; the resumed invocation result is `success:true`; the exported session contains `WOKE 0`; workload marker files contain `S11-WAKE-OK`.\n60: \n61: Runtime claim: S11 does not require a durable `state.db` schema migration.\n62: \n63: Proof method: touched-file inventory plus `crates/oulipoly-state/src/schema.rs`, `crates/oulipoly-state/src/migrations.rs`, `crates/oulipoly-state/src/db.rs::tests::has_session_user_text_turn_requires_exact_user_body_match`, and `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs::tests::mark_delivery_failed_records_attempt_without_delivery`.\n64: \n65: Evidence-class match: source invariant plus state/mailbox tests. The S11 touched list does not include schema or migration files; the current schema version remains unchanged, and the new behavior uses existing session-turn body reads plus existing sidecar mailbox columns.\n\n(End of file - total 65 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# S11 Gate Proposal\n\nS11 completes the external-provider wake cutover. The delta makes detached wake resumes use the same selected provider settings, policy, launch environment, registry root, and captured provider session identity as the original external launch. It also stops treating a spawned wake as delivered until delivery is confirmed by provider-ingested turn evidence or by a signed submitted-turn marker.\n\nThe implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. New wake ownership and delivery-attempt state lives in the existing sidecar mailbox/pid-identity database, while state-db reads are limited to existing session-turn/body data. The audited functional cutoff is `95699d6..1593ef8`; the audited net source includes the post-cutoff mode-only remediation `1e043d3`, because the pinned cutoff tracked `scripts/opencode-turns` as non-executable and could not pass the workspace test suite.\n\nLive evidence exists for external wake dispatch, spawned detached wake, resumed notification delivery, and successful xhigh external smoke. Live S10 files prove external launch smoke, while deterministic S10 tests remain the binding resume-compatibility proof.\n\n## Proof plan\n\nEvidence log: `planning/s11-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n\nRuntime claim: External provider launch records the provider child process identity and captured provider session id so `agent-messenger notify` can resolve the sidecar owner and spawn a detached wake.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_launch_notify_uses_captured_sidecar_owner_and_wakes` plus live `/tmp/s11-e2e/initial10.log` and `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-notify2.stdout`.\n\nEvidence-class match: full CLI/external-provider integration plus live smoke. The shipped test launches through the external provider fixture, captures sidecar identity, enqueues a notification, and asserts wake spawn. The live logs show `DISPATCHED ab_19e9eb3c740_11963_58a0b4405542b714`, successful initial `OULIPOLY_RESULT`, and wake `status: spawned` with `auto_wake_count: 1`.\n\nRuntime claim: A headless wake is not marked delivered merely because resume ran; delivery is confirmed only when the resumed provider output yields a matching submitted-turn marker or exact ingested user turn evidence.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/proposal.md","text":"# S11 Gate Proposal\n\nS11 completes the external-provider wake cutover. The delta makes detached wake resumes use the same selected provider settings, policy, launch environment, registry root, and captured provider session identity as the original external launch. It also stops treating a spawned wake as delivered until delivery is confirmed by provider-ingested turn evidence or by a signed submitted-turn marker.\n\nThe implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. New wake ownership and delivery-attempt state lives in the existing sidecar mailbox/pid-identity database, while state-db reads are limited to existing session-turn/body data. The audited functional cutoff is `95699d6..1593ef8`; the audited net source includes the post-cutoff mode-only remediation `1e043d3`, because the pinned cutoff tracked `scripts/opencode-turns` as non-executable and could not pass the workspace test suite.\n\nLive evidence exists for external wake dispatch, spawned detached wake, resumed notification delivery, and successful xhigh external smoke. Live S10 files prove external launch smoke, while deterministic S10 tests remain the binding resume-compatibility proof.\n\n## Proof plan\n\nEvidence log: `planning/s11-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n\nRuntime claim: External provider launch records the provider child process identity and captured provider session id so `agent-messenger notify` can resolve the sidecar owner and spawn a detached wake.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_launch_notify_uses_captured_sidecar_owner_and_wakes` plus live `/tmp/s11-e2e/initial10.log` and `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-notify2.stdout`.\n\nEvidence-class match: full CLI/external-provider integration plus live smoke. The shipped test launches through the external provider fixture, captures sidecar identity, enqueues a notification, and asserts wake spawn. The live logs show `DISPATCHED ab_19e9eb3c740_11963_58a0b4405542b714`, successful initial `OULIPOLY_RESULT`, and wake `status: spawned` with `auto_wake_count: 1`.\n\nRuntime claim: A headless wake is not marked delivered merely because resume ran; delivery is confirmed only when the resumed provider output yields a matching submitted-turn marker or exact ingested user turn evidence.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_wake_does_not_mark_delivered_when_resume_produces_no_turn`, `::external_provider_wake_confirms_delivery_from_submitted_turn_marker`, `::external_provider_wake_ignores_submitted_turn_marker_for_different_payload`, `src-tauri/tests/wu_b_mailbox_integration.rs::resume_marks_delivered_from_exact_ingested_user_turn_without_assistant_delta`, and `::resume_rejects_different_ingested_user_turn_without_assistant_delta`.\n\nEvidence-class match: integration tests exercise the exact resume/mailbox delivery path and state assertions, not a proxy. They cover the negative no-turn path, positive nonce/hash marker confirmation, wrong-payload rejection, and built-in exact-user-turn confirmation.\n\nRuntime claim: Failed or rate-limited external wake attempts remain pending, record delivery-attempt/error evidence, release the wake claim, and can be retried.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_failed_wake_releases_claim_and_retries_pending_mailbox`, `::external_provider_rate_limited_wake_records_error_and_retries_pending_mailbox`, and `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs::tests::mark_delivery_failed_records_attempt_without_delivery`.\n\nEvidence-class match: full wake integration plus sidecar unit coverage. The tests assert `delivery_attempts` increments, `delivered_at` remains absent on failure, `delivery_error` records the failure class, claims are released, and the pending mailbox row is eligible for retry.\n\nRuntime claim: External-provider policy and launch requests carry the selected provider settings identity, hybrid launch shape, host linkage environment, and account-specific OpenCode auth while excluding ambient OpenAI/XDG leakage.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/age217_s6a_policy_launch_dispatch.rs::external_provider_policy_evaluate_runs_before_launch_and_uses_selected_provider_settings`, `::external_provider_policy_request_passes_hybrid_launch_shape`, `::external_provider_launch_env_carries_host_linkage_without_openai_keys`, `::external_provider_launch_env_uses_selected_opencode_auth_context_without_openai_keys`, `::external_provider_launch_env_omits_ambient_xdg_for_default_opencode_auth_context`, and `::external_provider_policy_rejection_skips_launch`.\n\nEvidence-class match: executor/provider-client integration fixtures capture the actual policy and launch protocol requests, environment maps, and rejection branch. This directly validates the request-shaping and environment boundary being claimed.\n\nRuntime claim: Detached wake resume reloads the external provider registry from the launch-time model/config roots and can use ingested external sessions when the original launch capture is missing.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs::external_provider_launch_notify_uses_captured_sidecar_owner_and_wakes` and `::external_provider_runtime_uses_ingested_session_when_launch_capture_missing`.\n\nEvidence-class match: CLI/external-provider integration uses isolated model/config/data roots and proves the wake path supplies the correct models dir and session source. The fallback test exercises the runtime path where launch capture is absent but ingested external session metadata is available.\n\nRuntime claim: S11 preserves S10 external provider launch/resume compatibility.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`, `::external_launch_session_id_alias_persists_external_capture_method_without_session_capability`, and live `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log`, `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log`, `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-nes-projects-agent-runner/45ccb26a-8bb6-4486-9b1e-2226e29292a0/tasks/b1vgap7ge.output`.\n\nEvidence-class match: deterministic CLI integration remains the binding resume proof and asserts external launch resume with recorded cwd and capture rows. Live smoke is scoped to external launch success only: the files contain `S10-EXTERNAL-OK` or `S10-FINAL-OK` and successful result markers.\n\nRuntime claim: The external xhigh route still reaches the external provider path after S11.\n\nProof method: `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-nes-projects-agent-runner/45ccb26a-8bb6-4486-9b1e-2226e29292a0/tasks/bvnh4nja0.output`.\n\nEvidence-class match: live runtime smoke. The retained task output records `gpt-xhigh flipped`, `=== smoke: gpt-xhigh through external ===`, `XHIGH-EXTERNAL-OK`, and `\"status\":\"succeeded\"`.\n\nRuntime claim: Live S11 wake delivery reached the resumed provider and the sidecar recorded confirmed delivery in one attempt.\n\nProof method: `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-opencode-argv.log`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-xdg-data/oulipoly-agent-runner/pid-identity.db`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh10-xdg-data/oulipoly-agent-runner/invocations/7a46a1a5-844d-45e8-bd67-aecaf9cf9194.result`, `/tmp/s11-e2e/fresh13-export.json`, and workload marker files under `/tmp/s11-e2e`.\n\nEvidence-class match: live sidecar/runtime artifacts. The argv log shows the resumed prompt with `[OULIPOLY NOTIFICATIONS]`; the mailbox rows have `delivered_at` set, `delivered_by_invocation_uuid='7a46a1a5-844d-45e8-bd67-aecaf9cf9194'`, `delivery_attempts=1`, and `delivery_error=NULL`; the resumed invocation result is `success:true`; the exported session contains `WOKE 0`; workload marker files contain `S11-WAKE-OK`.\n\nRuntime claim: S11 does not require a durable `state.db` schema migration.\n\nProof method: touched-file inventory plus `crates/oulipoly-state/src/schema.rs`, `crates/oulipoly-state/src/migrations.rs`, `crates/oulipoly-state/src/db.rs::tests::has_session_user_text_turn_requires_exact_user_body_match`, and `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs::tests::mark_delivery_failed_records_attempt_without_delivery`.\n\nEvidence-class match: source invariant plus state/mailbox tests. The S11 touched list does not include schema or migration files; the current schema version remains unchanged, and the new behavior uses existing session-turn body reads plus existing sidecar mailbox columns.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":65,"totalLines":65,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/proposal.md","time":{"start":1780782348031,"end":1780782348081}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_02a7eae52b1f1412016a24950bf4f08193905123d5857db4b5"}},"id":"prt_e9ee636f9001zBOp0Yd5HK6PmM","sessionID":"ses_1611a223effeVm9QJZLOjnSHiy","messageID":"msg_e9ee5ded00018U4MXfBBrZzlIy"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780782348088,"sessionID":"ses_1611a223effeVm9QJZLOjnSHiy","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_zqPZTAbLrzVNrn9CPaFuGpHr","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/contracts/s11.contract.md","offset":1,"limit":4000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s11-gate/contracts/s11.contract.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # S11 Gate Contract\n2: \n3: ## Component declared roles\n4: \n5: Component: external provider wake delivery confirmation and detached resume continuity.\n6: \n7: Declared roles: `orchestration`, `accessor`, `mapper`, `filter`, `validator`, `predicate`, `formatter`, `parser`.\n8: \n9: ## Source scope\n10: \n11: The functional S11 cutoff is `95699d6..1593ef8`. The audited net source range is `95699d6..1e043d3` because `1593ef8` left `scripts/opencode-turns` tracked as non-executable, which made the workspace test suite fail in `age243_s7a_session_dispatch`. The only remaining `1593ef8..1e043d3` source delta is `mode change 100644 => 100755 scripts/opencode-turns`; `2b7ab03` content is reverted and not part of the net source behavior.\n12: \n13: `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/generated.rs` receives no generated-artifact exemption. It is treated as hand-maintained provider protocol DTO code for this gate, consistent with the S10B disposition.\n14: \n15: Touched files in scope are exactly `planning/s11-gate/gates/touched-files.txt`.\n16: \n17: ## Touched-file roles\n18: \n19: | File | Declared roles | Role notes |\n20: |---|---|---|\n21: | `DECISIONS.md` | `formatter` | Decision log context for validation/audit ratification; prose only. |\n22: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/client.rs` | `accessor`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | Provider client invoke flow, process observer plumbing, and timeout/default mapping. |\n23: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/error.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider error envelope parsing, typed diagnostics/status access, and error mapping. |\n24: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/generated.rs` | `formatter`, `mapper`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider protocol DTOs and fixed discriminator/boolean validation. |\n25: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/process.rs` | `accessor`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | Provider subprocess lifecycle and spawn-observer process identity capture. |\n26: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/cli.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | CLI executor environment/session orchestration and parent linkage handling. |\n27: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/cli/result.rs` | `mapper`, `predicate` | Execution result/session capture DTO mapping and presence predicates. |\n28: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/cli/spawn_identity.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `validator` | Process identity observation and sidecar owner/session persistence. |\n29: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/context.rs` | `mapper`, `accessor` | External dispatch context and selected provider settings identity mapping. |\n30: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/dispatch.rs` | `orchestration`, `mapper`, `accessor` | External provider policy/launch dispatch, process identity backfill, and result mapping. |\n31: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/error_formatter.rs` | `formatter`, `accessor` | Stable external provider terminal/diagnostic message formatting. |\n32: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/error_mapper.rs` | `mapper`, `predicate`, `formatter` | Provider-client/registry error mapping and rotatable/terminal classification. |\n33: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/errors.rs` | `formatter`, `mapper` | External provider dispatch error value and display surface. |\n34: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime